              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new run of words: what did nakṣatraṁ mean, and what did veyil mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: മരം",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "മരം (maraṁ) — \"a tree\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Inherited Dravidian, matching Tamil maram, Kannada mara and Telugu mrānu. Four languages, one word, and no borrowing anywhere in it — this is a word the family had before it split."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It carries the standing tree and the cut timber on one shape. A carpenter's മരം (maraṁ) and a forest's മരം (maraṁ) are the same word, and Malayalam leaves it to the situation to say which is meant. English needs tree and wood to do that work."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kerala's own tree, the coconut, gets a compound built on this one, and the ordinary word for a tree-climber is built on it too."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The thing the rest of this run takes apart."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
