        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: കൊമ്പ്",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കൊമ്പ് (kompŭ) — \"a branch\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Inherited, matching Tamil kompu. Malayalam puts one shape on anything that juts out and comes to a point: the branch of a tree, the horn of an ox, the tusk of an elephant, and the curved brass horn played at a temple festival."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is a picture doing the work rather than a rule. Once you have seen the branch and the horn as one thing, the elephant's tusk needs no separate word, and Malayalam never gave it one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The elephant sense is the one you are most likely to hear in Kerala, where the animal is a fixture of festival and of conversation both."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two: the tree, and the part that reaches out from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
